    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-800c-a22d-e656b7fcf5b6",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "5. Best Practices & Pitfalls"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-80ba-95ea-e17804a668c3",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Best Practices"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-80cf-a686-e4b5a99341e2",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Use specific method-level annotations like "
        ],
        [
          "@GetMapping",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ", "
        ],
        [
          "@PostMapping",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " for clarity instead of generic "
        ],
        [
          "@RequestMapping",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8073-a57f-d87218782be7",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Return meaningful HTTP status codes using "
        ],
        [
          "ResponseEntity",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " instead of just strings."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8006-b3c2-e8fccec80f38",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Validate request data and handle errors gracefully."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-80db-a515-d412e777429c",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Common Pitfalls"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8052-b656-ffc0cc27f558",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Forgetting to use "
        ],
        [
          "@RequestBody",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " for parsing JSON input in POST/PUT methods."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-80fc-aee1-f32ca0c54f2a",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Returning plain strings instead of JSON objects when a JSON response is expected."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8090-a8c5-d56501bfff27",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Not handling invalid path variables or input errors, causing server exceptions."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8001-b172-d70971648f73",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-809e-8a04-caa6d2f76f62",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "6. Connection to Other Concepts"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-806b-8ef0-d2af7b76328a",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Beans & Dependency Injection:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " Your controller class ("
        ],
        [
          "@RestController",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ") is managed as a Spring Bean, which means Spring creates and manages its lifecycle."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8000-8649-d6f0891336f6",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Auto-Configuration:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " Spring Boot auto-configures JSON converters (like Jackson) so you don’t have to manually set up JSON parsing."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8093-86a7-c1498c19a92e",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Exception Handling:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " You can connect REST controllers to Spring’s global exception handling ("
        ],
        [
          "@ControllerAdvice",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ") for cleaner error responses."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8022-a53b-c4fa9da613a7",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Security:",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " REST endpoints can be secured using Spring Security."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8049-8058-c70b96b71b23",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8088-96d1-ccb9fa397af3",
      "type": "sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "7. Quick Interview Answers"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-800f-a8f1-d1e875cca747",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: What is ",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "@RestController",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ],
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-80eb-b8a6-ffe79187d184",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "A: "
        ],
        [
          "@RestController",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " is a Spring Boot annotation that marks a class as a controller where every method returns domain objects instead of views. The objects are automatically converted to JSON or XML and sent back as HTTP responses, making it ideal for building RESTful APIs."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-80a3-8fc7-c93ab8cfc768",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: How do you map a REST API endpoint to a method?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-801b-b1e4-da9d7e9dfaaf",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "A: Using "
        ],
        [
          "@RequestMapping",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " or its shortcuts like "
        ],
        [
          "@GetMapping",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " and "
        ],
        [
          "@PostMapping",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ", you define the URL path and HTTP method that should trigger a Java method in your controller."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-809b-a6c7-e52f4a7394d1",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Q: How does Spring Boot convert Java objects into JSON?",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8001-afef-c604741af6bf",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "A: Spring Boot includes a JSON converter (usually Jackson) that automatically serializes Java objects returned from REST controller methods into JSON format for HTTP responses."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8090-8ccc-dca92eeac2ed",
      "type": "divider"
    }
  ]
}
